 To add IDs to the HTML elements in your React component, you can use the `htmlFor` attribute for labels and the `id` attribute for other elements. Here's how you can modify your code:

```jsx
export const CreateObjectView = observer(({ model }: { model: CreateObjectModel }) => {
  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="asset-select">Asset:</label>
      <select id="asset-select" value={model.selectedAsset} onChange={(e) => model.setSelectedAsset(e.target.value)}>
        <option value="" />
        {model.availableAssets.map((asset) => (
          <option key={asset.id} value={asset.id}>
            {asset.name}
          </option>
        ))}
      </select>

      <button onClick={() => { if (model.selectedAsset) { model.createObject(model.selectedAsset).then(() => model.setSelectedAsset(model.selectedAsset)); model.cleanupScene(); }}}>
        Add Object
      </button>

      <button id="cleanup-scene-btn" onClick={model.cleanupScene}>Cleanup scene</button>
    </div>
  );
});
```

In this example, I added an `id` attribute to the `select` element with the name "asset-select", and an `id` attribute to the "Cleanup scene" button with a descriptive name "cleanup-scene-btn". The options in the dropdown list now have their own unique keys based on the asset's `id`. This way, you can easily reference these elements from CSS or when writing tests.

