 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebApi project, follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` NuGet package for handling JWT tokens in your project. If not, install it using the following command in the Package Manager Console:

   ```
   Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
   ```

2. Create a method to validate and extract the username from the JWT token. Add this code to a new or existing class in your project, usually in a separate helper file or within the `AuthFilters` folder if you're using an `AuthorizationFilterContext`.

   ```csharp
   using System;
   using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;

   public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(string jwtToken)
   {
       try
       {
           var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
           var jsonToken = handler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken);

           return jsonToken.Claims.FirstOrDefault(x => x.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;
       }
       catch (Exception ex)
       {
           throw new ArgumentException("Invalid JWT token", nameof(jwtToken));
       }
   }
   ```

3. Now you can use this method in your controller actions to get the username from the JWT token. For example, create a new controller with a simple action that accepts the JWT token as a parameter and returns the corresponding username.

   ```csharp
