 In computer programming, particularly in functional programming and object-oriented programming, the terms "inner functions" and "outer functions" refer to specific relationships between functions.

An outer function is the function in which an inner function is defined. An inner function is a function that's defined inside another function. Inner functions have access to the variables of their outer function, even after the outer function has returned. This feature is also known as "closure".

Here's a simple example in JavaScript:

```javascript
function outerFunction(x) {
  return function innerFunction(y) {
    return x + y;
  };
}

const addFive = outerFunction(5); // create an inner function closed over x (which is 5)
console.log(addFive(3)); // prints 8
```

In this example, `outerFunction` is an outer function, and it returns an inner function named `innerFunction`. The inner function takes one argument `y`, and when called with an argument, it adds the value of `x` (which is 5 in this case) to the given value of `y`. Even though `outerFunction` has already returned, the inner function still retains access to `x`.

