 To call a PowerShell script from Terraform with an interactive `Read-Host` input, you cannot directly achieve this using standard Terraform functionality as Terraform is designed to be infrastructure automation tool and does not support interactive inputs during execution. However, you can create workarounds using the following methods:

1. **Environment Variables:** Set the required input values as environment variables before running the PowerShell script. Update your Terraform configuration file with `local_file` or `file` resource to set the environment variables.

```hcl
locals {
  env_variable = jsonencode({
    VAR1 = "value1"
    VAR2 = "value2"
  })
}

resource "local_file" "env_script" {
  content     = "set-content Env:MyVariablePath -Value '${local.env_variable}'"
  filename    = "C:/path/to/your/script.ps1"
  provisioner "file" {
    source      = "${path.module}/scripts/setenv.ps1"
    destination = "/tmp"
  }
  provisioner "remote-exec" {
    inline = [
      "powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File /tmp/setenv.ps1",
      "powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:/path/to/your/script.ps1"
    ]
  }
}
```
Replace `VAR1`, `VAR2`, `value1`, `value2`, and `C:/path/to/your/script.ps1` with your actual variable names, values, and PowerShell script path.

2. **Use PowerCLI:** If you're working in a PowerCLI environment like vSphere, you can pass input parameters through PowerCLI and pass them to your PowerShell script. Update your Terraform configuration file with `PowerCLI` provider and `PowerCLI Script` resource.

```hcl
provider "vmware" {
  version = "3.0.4"
}

data "vsphere_datacenter" "dc" {}
data "vsphere_cluster" "cluster" {
  name          = "ClusterName"

