 I'd be happy to help you understand a logical syllogism with an example! A syllogism is a kind of argument that consists of two propositions, called the premises, and a conclusion that logically follows from those premises. Here's an example of a classic syllogism:

\begin{align*}
&\text{All men are mortal.} \\
&\text{Socrates is a man.} \\
&\therefore \text{Socrates is mortal.}
\end{align*}

This syllogism is known as "Barbara" in the tradition of Aristotle. The first proposition, "All men are mortal," is the general statement or major premise. The second proposition, "Socrates is a man," is the particular statement or minor premise. The conclusion, "Socrates is mortal," follows logically from the premises because if all men are mortal and Socrates is a man, then it must follow that Socrates is mortal.

The logical structure of this argument can be represented using the following schema:

\begin{align*}
&\text{All }A\text{'s are }B \\
&\text{C is an }A \\
&\therefore \text{C is }B
\end{align*}

In our example, \(A\) stands for "men," \(B\) stands for "mortal," and \(C\) stands for "Socrates."

