 To check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric in R, you can use the `sapply()` function along with the `as.numeric()` and `is.na()` functions. Here's an example of how to write the R code:

```R
# Example character vector
char_vector <- c("1", "2.5", "three", NA, "4", "five")

# Function to check conversion to numeric
check_numeric <- function(x) {
  if (is.na(x)) return(FALSE)
  if (class(as.numeric(x)) == "numeric") return(TRUE)
  else return(FALSE)
}

# Apply function to each element in character vector
numeric_elements <- sapply(char_vector, check_numeric)

# Check if all elements are numeric
if (all(numeric_elements)) {
  cat("All non-NA elements in the character vector can be converted to numeric.\n")
} else {
  cat("Not all non-NA elements in the character vector can be converted to numeric.\n")
}
```

In this example, the `check_numeric()` function checks whether each element in the character vector can be converted to numeric by applying `as.numeric()` and checking its class. The `sapply()` function applies the `check_numeric()` function to each element in the character vector and returns a logical vector indicating whether each element is numeric or not. Finally, we use the `all()` function to check if all elements in the logical vector are `TRUE`.

