To achieve this, you can use GridFS, which is an extension for storing and retrieving large files, including images, in MongoDB. It automatically splits the file into chunks and stores each chunk as a separate document in a special collection called `fs.files` along with its corresponding metadata in another collection called `fs.chunks`. When you want to retrieve the image, MongoDB will reassemble the chunks and return the file content to your application.

Here's a simple example using Node.js and Mongoose library:

1. Install required packages:
```
npm install mongoose multer gridfs-stream
```

2. Set up your Mongoose schema and model:

```javascript
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
const multer = require('multer');
const GridFsStorage = require('gridfs-storage');
const Grid = require('gridfs');

// Initialize connection to MongoDB
mongoose.connect('mongodb://localhost:27017/test', {useNewUrlParser: true});

// Create custom storage engine for handling files
const conn = mongoose.connection;
const gfs = Grid(conn);
const storage = new GridFsStorage({url: 'mongodb://localhost:27017/test', file: (req, file) => `${file.originalname}-${Date.now()}`});
const upload = multer({storage});

// Define schema and model for your document
const ImageSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
  name: String,
  image: { type: mongoose.Schema.Types.Buffer, contentType: String },
});

ImageSchema.plugin(require('mongoose-gridfs-image'));
const Image = mongoose.model('Image', ImageSchema);

module.exports = { Image, gfs, ImageSchema, upload };

