response:
 Based on the given code snippet, it appears that the issue is with the generation of random positions for the planets such that they end up being too close to each other. One possible solution to address this would be to implement a rejection mechanism when generating a new planet position. This can be achieved by keeping track of the current positions of all existing planets and checking for collisions before adding a new planet. If a collision is detected, then generate a new random position and repeat the process until a suitable position is found.

Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function with this approach:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = []
  let attempts = 0

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    }

    let tooClose = false

    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const existingPlanet = planets[i]
      for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {
        const dist = Math.abs(planet.position[j] - existingPlanet.position[j])
        if (dist < minDistance) {
          tooClose = true
          break
        }
      }
      if (tooClose) {
        break
      }
    }

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet)
    }
  }

  return planets
}
